Thrown from the method Default outcome <code>RuntimeException</code> (unchecked) Rollback. Unchecked exceptions usually mean a bug or an unexpected failure, so the data may be inconsistent. <code>Error</code> Rollback. <code>OutOfMemoryError</code> and the like mean the JVM is in trouble. Checked <code>Exception</code> (e.g. <code>IOException</code>, <code>SQLException</code> thrown directly) Commit. Spring follows the old EJB convention that checked exceptions are expected business outcomes the caller should handle, not failures of the unit of work. This surprises almost everyone. Exception caught inside the method and not rethrown Commit. The proxy only sees what leaves the method, and if you swallow the exception, as far as it knows everything succeeded. <pre><code>@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)          // roll back on checked exceptions too (a common team-wide convention)
public void importFile(Path p) throws IOException { ... }

@Transactional(noRollbackFor = DuplicateEmailException.class) // this runtime exception is an expected outcome: keep the other changes
public void register(User u) { ... }
</code></pre> <p>Note that Spring's own data-access exceptions (<code>DataAccessException</code> and its subclasses) are unchecked, so failures from repositories and <code>JdbcTemplate</code> roll back by default.</p>

Annotation Loads Use for Plain JUnit + Mockito Nothing: no Spring at all. You construct the class with <code>new</code> and pass mocks. Most service logic. These run in milliseconds, which is one more benefit of constructor injection. <code>@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)</code> Only the MVC layer: the controller(s), <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code>, filters, converters, Jackson config, security config. Not services or repositories; you must mock them. Provides a <code>MockMvc</code> for sending fake requests without a real server. Request mapping, validation, status codes, error responses, JSON shape, security rules on URLs. <code>@DataJpaTest</code> JPA repositories, <code>EntityManager</code>, Flyway/Liquibase migrations, and an embedded database by default. Each test runs in a transaction that rolls back afterwards. Custom queries, entity mappings, constraints. <code>@JsonTest</code> Only Jackson configuration (<code>ObjectMapper</code>, <code>@JsonComponent</code>s). Serialisation and deserialisation details: date formats, field names, polymorphic types. <code>@SpringBootTest</code> The full application context, everything. Add <code>webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT</code> to start a real server on a free port and call it over real HTTP. Integration tests of whole flows. The slowest option, so use it sparingly. <p>The <code>@WebMvcTest</code>, <code>@DataJpaTest</code> and <code>@JsonTest</code> annotations are called test slices: each enables only the auto-configuration relevant to one layer, so the context starts fast and a failure points at that layer.</p> <pre><code>@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)                  // MVC slice: only UserController and web infrastructure
class UserControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;                         // sends requests through the full MVC stack, without a real server
    @MockitoBean UserService service;               // Boot 3.4+ (Spring 6.2): a Mockito mock registered as a bean; earlier versions use @MockBean

    @Test
    void rejectsInvalidEmail() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(post("/users").contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("""
                    {"name":"Ana","email":"not-an-email","age":30}
                    """))
           .andExpect(status().isBadRequest());     // validation failed before the controller ran; the service was never called
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>The test proves the URL mapping, JSON parsing, validation annotations and error handling together, without touching a database. If security is configured, <code>@WebMvcTest</code> includes it, so add <code>@WithMockUser</code> (or a JWT request post-processor) to simulate an authenticated user, and <code>.with(csrf())</code> for POSTs in session-based apps.</p>
